kosha projection

  -- the method of representing a 3D object on a 2D drawing surface.
  -- the fundamental act of engineering drawing: collapsing 3D to 2D systematically.
  --
  -- orthographic projection: parallel rays of light. no perspective distortion.
  --   viewer is at infinity — parallel lines stay parallel.
  --   produces true shape and size on each view — measurable.
  --   multiple views needed to fully define 3D object.
  --
  -- first-angle projection (ISO, Europe, Asia):
  --   object between viewer and projection plane.
  --   front view: viewer looks from front → image on plane behind object.
  --   top view placed BELOW front view (projected downward).
  --   right-side view placed to the LEFT of front view.
  --   symbol: circle with cone pointing left.
  --
  -- third-angle projection (ASME, USA, Canada):
  --   projection plane between viewer and object.
  --   top view placed ABOVE front view (where it was seen from).
  --   right-side view placed to the RIGHT of front view.
  --   symbol: circle with cone pointing right.
  --   more intuitive (view placed where viewer stood).
  --
  -- isometric projection: 3D view. axes at 120° to each other.
  --   all three dimensions visible simultaneously.
  --   foreshortening factor 0.816 — in isometric drawing, full size used.
  --   parallel lines remain parallel (not perspective).
  --   exploded views: parts pulled apart along isometric axes.
  --
  -- section view: object cut by a plane to show internal features.
  --   cutting plane line shows where cut is made.
  --   hatching (angled lines) shows cut surfaces — material cross-section.
  --   full section, half section, revolved section, removed section.

  "drawing-varga-vishesa"
  "engineering-varga-vishesa"
  "subanta-swarupa"

  "geometry-yukta"
  "orthographic-swarupa isometric-swarupa"
  "coordinate-yukta"
  "domain-3d-yukta"          -- CAD projections are automated versions of this

